mainmenu "Microchip XEC tachometer PWM loopback"

config APP_RAW_COUNT_MODE
	bool "Measure the latched count instead of sweeping the PWM"
	help
	  Replace the PWM loopback self test with a measurement of the count the
	  tachometer latches, averaged over many readings, and of the per latch
	  offset between that count and the number of 100 kHz clocks the
	  measurement window took.

	  This needs an external generator driving the tachometer inputs rather
	  than the on-chip PWM: a PWM output derived from the same 100 kHz clock
	  the tachometer counts places every input edge on a counter clock edge,
	  which is exactly the coincidence the measurement has to avoid. Select
	  it through raw-count.conf, which comes with the overlay that keeps the
	  PWM off the shared pin.

config APP_RAW_COUNT_READINGS
	int "Latched counts averaged per instance"
	default 200
	range 4 1000
	depends on APP_RAW_COUNT_MODE
	help
	  The counter truncates, so a single reading is only ever within one
	  count of the true window width. Averaging over readings taken at
	  different phases of the 100 kHz clock recovers the fraction.

	  The default of 200 is what the two runs this measurement was made
	  with used, and they returned the three pair estimates spread over
	  0.13 and 0.21 counts against the quarter count the sample requires
	  them to agree to. 50 leaves too little margin: the estimator is
	  differencing two averages, so its error is larger than that of
	  either.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
